 state we can  again write out  ni then annihilate the particle  and then create it again what these two  relations show is that the fox states  are the eigenstate of the occupation  number operator  and the eigenvalues simply tell us how  many particles are in that state  now for fermions we have been discussing  that we have to be careful with the  order of the u terms  so you may think that i tricked you here  by conveniently placing the state i'm  annihilating and creating in the first  entry already  it turns out that the result is actually  valid irrespective of the order  this is because if the ui state starts  somewhere in the list that is not the  first position like this  what we have to do then is to perform p  exchanges  and these p exchanges result in a factor  of minus one to the p  to get it to the first place then we can  apply the occupation number operator to  recover the same state  and then we perform another p exchanges  so another factor of -1 to the p  to take it back where it started this  means that we have two factors of -1 to  the p  so that the overall factor is minus 1 to  the 2p and this is always equal to 1.  this means that there is never an  overall minus sign and the occupation  number operator really does count the  number of occupied states  with this result we can now construct  the operator associated with the total  number of particles  like this or again explicitly in terms  of creation  and annihilation operators  to finish let's compare the fermionic  creation and annihilation operators  with the corresponding bosonic operators  and for the latter you can find a  detailed discussion in the companion  video linked in the description  what we have found in this video is that  the creation and annihilation operators  for fermions obey these anti-commutation  relations  which are a direct consequence of the  anti-symmetric nature of fermionic  states  by contrast in the video on bosonic  creation and annihilation operators  we find that they instead obey  commutation relations  and again these are a consequence of the  symmetric nature of bosonic states  the anti-commutation algebra of  fermionic creation annihilation  operators captures  all the subtleties associated with the  anti-symmetry of fermionic states  this forms the basis of describing  fermions in second quantization  don't forget to check the companion  video on bosons if you haven't yet  and if you liked the video please  subscribe
 

